---
import DashboardPreview from "../components/DashboardPreview.astro";
import JarvisLockup from "../components/JarvisLockup.astro";
import PageHead from "../components/PageHead.astro";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { getPage } from "../lib/site";

const page = getPage("/product");

const modes = [
  { name: "Plan", text: "Read and plan. Nothing is modified; writes, commands and network changes are denied or asked." },
  { name: "Approve", text: "The default. Safe work proceeds; anything that needs your authority pauses for a decision." },
  { name: "Auto", text: "Actions covered by your rules run automatically. Everything else still asks." },
  { name: "Bypass", text: "Broad local authority, only when you choose it, with a persistent indicator. Pushes, deploys and spending still follow their own rules." },
  { name: "Custom", text: "Named rule sets you define, such as a read-only reviewer or a local builder." },
];
---
<Base title={page.title} description={page.description} path={page.path}>
  <PageHead
    title="One command center for the coding agents you already use."
    lead="KalCode is a desktop workspace for developers who work with AI coding agents. This page describes what it is being built to do."
    devNote="KalCode is in private development. Nothing here is available to download yet."
  >
    <div class="button-row">
      <a class="button button--primary" href="/download">Join early access</a>
      <a class="button button--secondary" href="#dashboard">See the Dashboard design</a>
    </div>
  </PageHead>

  <section class="section" aria-label="What KalCode does">
    <div class="container feature-list">
      <article class="feature" id="providers">
        <h2 class="h2">Bring your providers.</h2>
        <div class="feature__body">
          <p>
            KalCode connects to Claude Code, Codex and Gemini CLI through their documented
            integration methods: your own signed-in local CLI, or an API key you provide. It never
            copies hidden tokens or scrapes browser sessions.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Connecting a provider is free on every plan, including Free.</li>
            <li>Each provider runs in its own supervised process, so one failing never stops the others.</li>
            <li>No single provider is a dependency: the architecture treats them all the same way.</li>
          </ul>
          <a class="text-link" href="/docs/providers">How provider connections work</a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="feature" id="threads">
        <h2 class="h2">Threads in real workspaces.</h2>
        <div class="feature__body">
          <p>
            A thread is a persistent unit of AI work. It runs in a real project directory with a real
            terminal, and it keeps its history so you can stop, resume and review it later.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Code workspace puts threads next to the tools you use to check them: terminal,
            files, Git, and diffs.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="feature" id="permissions">
        <h2 class="h2">Permissions you set, for every agent.</h2>
        <div class="feature__body">
          <p>
            Every action an agent wants to take is classified and checked against your permission
            mode before it runs. When a decision is needed, you choose: deny, approve once, approve
            for the thread or workspace, or allow it with a rule.
          </p>
          <dl class="mode-list">
            {
              modes.map((mode) => (
                <div>
                  <dt>{mode.name}</dt>
                  <dd>{mode.text}</dd>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </dl>
          <a class="text-link" href="/docs/permissions">Read the permission model</a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="feature" id="jarvis">
        <div class="feature__lead">
          <h2 class="h2">JARVIS coordinates, you decide.</h2>
          <JarvisLockup sizes="(min-width: 56rem) 18rem, min(calc(100vw - 5rem), 18rem)" />
        </div>
        <div class="feature__body">
          <p>
            JARVIS is the intelligence inside KalCode. Talk to it and it can plan an objective,
            split it into threads, delegate each one to the provider that fits, and bring the results
            back together.
          </p>
          <p>
            Larger objectives become missions: multi-step work that is not called done until there is
            verification evidence, such as tests that ran or checks that passed.
          </p>
          <p>
            JARVIS operates inside your permission model. It cannot raise its own authority or turn on
            Bypass.
          </p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/docs/jarvis">How JARVIS is designed</a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="feature" id="local-first">
        <h2 class="h2">Local-first by default.</h2>
        <div class="feature__body">
          <p>
            Projects, threads, events, settings and memory stay on your device. Credentials are
            stored in your operating system's keychain and never written to the database, logs or
            the interface.
          </p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/docs/local-first">What stays on your device</a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band section preview-band" id="dashboard" data-theme="dark" aria-labelledby="dashboard-title">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2 id="dashboard-title" class="h2">The Dashboard: runtime truth.</h2>
        <p>
          Status on the Dashboard comes from structured runtime events — a thread started, a tool
          was called, an approval was requested — never from a model's own account of its progress.
        </p>
      </div>
      <DashboardPreview />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="cta-title">
    <div class="container cta">
      <h2 id="cta-title" class="h2">Be first to try it.</h2>
      <p class="lead">Join early access and we'll email you when there is a build to try.</p>
      <a class="button button--primary" href="/download">Join early access</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
